No NuGet packages or external DLLs required — everything runs from source.


🚀 Getting Started
1. Clone the repo
git clone https://github.com/<deepanshu2singh>/godotsim.git

cd godotsim
2. Open in Godot
Open Godot 4 (Mono/C# build)
Select Import Project and point it at the godotsim/ folder
Godot will build the C# solution automatically on first open
3. Run the simulation
Press F5 or click Play in the Godot editor
In the UI:
Set Agent Count (e.g. 50)
Set Trial Runs (e.g. 10)
Adjust Speed slider (1x–5x)
Click Start
4. Find your output files
Output CSVs are saved to Godot's user:// data directory:

OS
Path
Windows
%APPDATA%\Godot\app_userdata\GodotSim\
macOS
~/Library/Application Support/Godot/app_userdata/GodotSim/
Linux
~/.local/share/godot/app_userdata/GodotSim/



📊 Sample Results
50-trial batch (N=50 agents, μ=0.8):

Metric

1) Avg Wait Time
GodotSim: Matches theoretical
SimPy: Matches theoretical
2) Avg System Time
GodotSim: Matches theoretical
SimPy: Matches theoretical
3)  Runtime (ms)
GodotSim: Slightly higher (animation overhead)
SimPy: Faster (text-only)
4) Visual Output
GodotSim: ✅ Animated
SimPy: ❌ None


GodotSim produces statistically equivalent results to SimPy across 200-trial experiments, with modest additional runtime due to animation rendering — a justified trade-off for interpretability.


🔬 Simulation Parameters
The M/M/1 model uses:

Symbol
Meaning
Default
λ (Lambda)
Arrival rate (agents/sec)
1.0
μ (Mu)
Service rate (agents/sec)
0.8
ρ (Rho)
Server utilisation = λ/μ
1.25


Interarrival and service times are sampled from exponential distributions using the PCG random generator (via SimSharp).


🗺️ Roadmap
M/M/c multi-server queues — extend SimSharpEngine to support multiple Resource instances
Priority queues and reneging — add customer classes and timeout-based abandonment
Live in-simulation dashboard — real-time Godot UI charts (wait time, utilisation) during playback
3D environment — upgrade from 2D sprites to Godot spatial nodes (hospital, airport, factory models)
VR support — OpenXR plugin integration for immersive simulation walkthroughs
IoT / live data — connect to real-world data streams for digital twin scenarios


📄 Academic Context
This project was submitted as an MSc dissertation at the University of Surrey (Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences) in September 2025. The research questions addressed:

Can a game engine serve as a viable real-time visualisation layer for DES?
Does animation overhead meaningfully degrade simulation performance?
What is the pedagogical and practical value of visual DES for non-technical users?

Key finding: Yes — with a design-science methodology and source-level integration, Godot can host a fully functional, visually immersive DES with competitive performance.


